## Why each additional element was added

**Second server:** Added to create redundancy. If one server goes down,
the other continues to handle requests. Both servers contain the same
stack: a web server (Nginx), an application server, a code base, and a
MySQL database. This eliminates the single server bottleneck from the
previous infrastructure.

**Load balancer (HAProxy):** Added to distribute incoming traffic between
the two servers. Without it, there would be no way to split the load or
route requests to a healthy server if one goes down.

**MySQL Primary-Replica cluster:** Added to separate read and write
operations, improve performance, and provide a database backup mechanism.
The Primary handles all writes; the Replica stays synchronized and
handles reads.

## Load balancer configuration

The load balancer is configured with a **Round Robin** algorithm. Each
incoming request is sent to the next server in rotation: request 1 goes
to Server 1, request 2 goes to Server 2, request 3 goes to Server 1
again, and so on. This ensures an even distribution of traffic when both
servers have similar capacity.

## Active-Active vs Active-Passive

This setup uses an **Active-Active** configuration. Both servers are
running simultaneously and both handle traffic at the same time. The load
balancer splits requests between them.

In an **Active-Passive** setup, only one server handles traffic at a
time. The second server stays idle and only activates if the first one
fails. Active-Active makes better use of available resources but requires
both servers to stay in sync. Active-Passive is simpler to manage but
wastes half the infrastructure capacity during normal operation.

## How a Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) database cluster works

The **Primary node** is the only node that accepts both read and write
operations (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, SELECT). Every change made on the
Primary is automatically replicated to the **Replica node** using MySQL
binary logs. The Replica applies these changes to stay synchronized with
the Primary in near real-time.

The **Replica node** is read-only. It cannot accept write operations. Its
role is to serve read queries and to act as a backup in case the Primary
fails. If the Primary goes down, the Replica can be manually or
automatically promoted to become the new Primary.

## Difference between Primary and Replica for the application

The application sends all **write queries** (creating a user, placing an
order, updating a record) to the **Primary node**. It sends **read
queries** (loading a profile, browsing a product list) to the **Replica
node**. This reduces the load on the Primary and improves overall
database performance. If the Primary fails, write operations become
impossible until it is restored or the Replica is promoted.

## Issues with this infrastructure

### SPOF (Single Points of Failure)

The **load balancer** is a single point of failure. If HAProxy goes down,
no request can reach either server and the entire website becomes
unreachable. There is no redundant load balancer to take over.

The **Primary database** is also a single point of failure for write
operations. If it goes down, no data can be written to the database until
it is restored or the Replica is promoted manually.

### Security issues

**No firewall:** all ports on all servers are exposed directly to the
internet. An attacker can attempt to connect to the database port (3306),
the application server, or any other service without any filtering.

**No HTTPS:** traffic between the user and the load balancer is not
encrypted. Passwords, session tokens, and sensitive user data travel in
plain text and can be intercepted by a man-in-the-middle attack.

### No monitoring

There is no monitoring tool in place (no Datadog, no Nagios, no
NewRelic, no Uptime Robot). If a server goes down, runs out of disk
space, or has its CPU overloaded, no alert is triggered. Problems can go
undetected for a long time, causing silent failures or data loss without
anyone being notified.